13. How can we create threads in java other than runnable/Thread class ?
1. callable
Callable is similar to Runnable, but it can return a result and throw exceptions.Future is used to retrieve the result of an asynchronous computation.
2. ExecutorService 
3. CompletableFuture

Earlier, Java provided Thread and Runnable for concurrency. Nowadays, we usually use ExecutorService, which manages a thread pool and reuses threads instead of
creating a new thread for every task. We submit Runnable or Callable tasks to it. Runnable doesn't return a result, while Callable can return a value and throw 
checked exceptions. When a Callable is submitted, it returns a Future, which can be used to retrieve the result using get(), although get() blocks until the task 
completes. Java 8 introduced CompletableFuture, which allows asynchronous programming without blocking. It supports chaining operations with methods like thenApply(),
thenAccept(), and thenCompose(), making it ideal for calling multiple independent services in parallel, such as in microservices.

| ExecutorService            | CompletableFuture                                                         |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Manages thread pools       | Builds asynchronous workflows                                             |
| Executes Runnable/Callable | Executes async tasks and chains operations                                |
| Uses Future                | Uses CompletableFuture                                                    |
| `Future.get()` blocks      | `thenApply()`, `thenAccept()`, `thenCompose()` are non-blocking callbacks |
| Good for background tasks  | Good for async APIs and microservices                                     |


We usually don't create threads directly. We submit tasks to thread management frameworks like ExecutorService, Callable, and CompletableFuture. 
These internally use threads from a thread pool.

The interviewer is checking whether you know modern Java concurrency.

1. Thread Class (Traditional)
class MyThread extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Running...");
    }
}

new MyThread().start();
Creates a new thread.
Cannot return a value.
Not preferred nowadays.
2. Runnable (Traditional)
Runnable task = () -> {
    System.out.println("Running...");
};

Thread t = new Thread(task);
t.start();
Better than extending Thread because Java supports single inheritance.
Cannot return a result.
Cannot throw checked exceptions.
3. Callable (Modern)
What is Callable?

Callable is just like Runnable but with two extra capabilities.

Returns a value.
Can throw checked exceptions.

Instead of

void run()

Callable has

V call() throws Exception

Example

Callable<Integer> task = () -> {
    return 10 + 20;
};

Notice

return 30;

Runnable cannot do this.

But can we directly execute Callable?

No.

You cannot do

new Thread(callable);

Because

Thread accepts only Runnable.

So we need

ExecutorService

4. ExecutorService

This is one of the most asked interview topics.

What is ExecutorService?

Think like this.

Suppose your company receives

1000 requests.

If every request creates a new Thread

Request1 -> Thread1
Request2 -> Thread2
Request3 -> Thread3
...
Request1000 -> Thread1000

Creating thousands of threads is expensive.

Instead Java creates

Pool

Thread1
Thread2
Thread3
Thread4
Thread5

Whenever a request comes

Task1 -> Thread1

Task2 -> Thread2

Task3 -> Thread1 (reused)

Task4 -> Thread4

The same threads are reused.

This is called

Thread Pool

ExecutorService manages this thread pool.

Example
ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

executor.submit(() -> {
    System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());
});

executor.shutdown();

Output

pool-1-thread-1

You didn't create any Thread manually.

ExecutorService created and managed it.

Callable + ExecutorService
ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

Callable<Integer> task = () -> {
    return 100;
};

Future<Integer> future = executor.submit(task);

System.out.println(future.get());

executor.shutdown();

Output

100
What is Future?

When you submit a Callable,

it starts executing in another thread.

But the result is not immediately available.

Future acts like a placeholder.

Task Running...

↓

Future

↓

Result Ready

↓

future.get()

Example

Future<Integer> future = executor.submit(task);

Later

Integer result = future.get();

It waits until the task finishes.

ExecutorService Interview Answer

ExecutorService is an interface used to manage a pool of worker threads. Instead of creating a new thread for every task, we submit tasks to ExecutorService.
It assigns an available thread from the pool, improving performance by reusing threads and reducing thread creation overhead. It supports executing Runnable and 
Callable tasks.

This answer is enough for most interviews.

5. CompletableFuture

This is asked in Java 8+ interviews.

Why was CompletableFuture introduced?

Suppose we use Future.

Future<Integer> future = executor.submit(task);

To get the result

future.get();

Problem

future.get()

blocks the current thread.

If the task takes

10 seconds

your thread waits for

10 seconds

Not good.

CompletableFuture solves this.

It lets you execute code

after the result is available

without blocking.

Example

CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
    return "Hello";
}).thenAccept(result -> {
    System.out.println(result);
});

Output

Hello

Notice

No

future.get();

No waiting.

When the task finishes,

Java automatically calls

thenAccept()

Another Example

CompletableFuture<Integer> future =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 20);

future.thenApply(num -> num * 2)
      .thenAccept(System.out::println);

Output

40

Flow

20

↓

Multiply by 2

↓

40

↓

Print
Real-world Example

Imagine an e-commerce application.

To display the product page, you need:

Product details
Inventory
Reviews

If each API call takes 2 seconds:

Without parallel execution:

Product → 2s
Inventory → 2s
Reviews → 2s

Total = 6 seconds

With CompletableFuture:

CompletableFuture<Product> product =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(this::getProduct);

CompletableFuture<Inventory> inventory =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(this::getInventory);

CompletableFuture<Reviews> reviews =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(this::getReviews);

CompletableFuture.allOf(product, inventory, reviews).join();

All three tasks run concurrently, so the total time is roughly 2 seconds instead of 6 seconds (assuming enough threads are available).

